<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  MB-350 — the splash logo, sized to survive the splash screen's circular mask.

  The theme pointed windowSplashScreenAnimatedIcon straight at @drawable/ic_logo,
  which is the 16:9 badge+wordmark lockup (640x360). The splash API masks that
  icon to the system icon shape — a circle — so a wide image loses both ends and
  the user sees the logo clipped into a horizontal oval.

  The geometry: the icon canvas is 240dp and only a 160dp-diameter circle is
  visible. A 16:9 rectangle fits inside a circle of diameter D when its diagonal
  equals D, so w = D / sqrt(1 + (9/16)^2) = 160 / 1.147 = 139dp. 132x74dp is that
  bound with margin to spare (diagonal 151dp against the 160dp circle), and it
  keeps the exact 16:9 ratio so the artwork is never distorted.

  The transparent 240dp square underneath is load-bearing, not padding. Without
  it this drawable's intrinsic size would be the bitmap's, and the platform
  scales the icon drawable to fill the canvas — which would blow the logo back up
  to the full width and re-crop it. The square pins the intrinsic aspect to 1:1
  so scale-to-canvas preserves the inset instead of eating it.

  Kept as the full lockup rather than swapping to the square ic_logo_mark: the
  request was for all of the logo with no cuts, and the mark alone would drop the
  wordmark. The cost is that the logo renders smaller than it used to.
-->
<layer-list xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <item>
        <shape android:shape="rectangle">
            <size android:width="240dp" android:height="240dp" />
            <solid android:color="@android:color/transparent" />
        </shape>
    </item>
    <item android:gravity="center">
        <bitmap
            android:src="@drawable/ic_logo"
            android:gravity="center"
            android:width="132dp"
            android:height="74dp" />
    </item>
</layer-list>
